introduced ASP. NET webforms in 2002. This made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:50.250
creation of web page similar to the creation&nbsp;
of Windows form. There are a few downsides with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:58.200
web forms, web forms, tried to make stateless web&nbsp;
stateful. And in order to do that, it had to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:07.560
lots of changes to make sure web pages retain&nbsp;
their state or their values automatically. View&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:14.760
states contained those values, and that resulted&nbsp;
in a lot of data being generated and transferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:22.350
with each request. Sometimes even if it was&nbsp;
not required. webforms was the first version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:30.720
to introduce code behind code behind is good, but&nbsp;
it makes testing difficult. Because in one line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:38.880
we are reading from the database, and in the next&nbsp;
line, we are updating the UI. Another common issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:46.950
was page life cycle. When requesting the page,&nbsp;
number of events were getting fired. Getting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:53.400
know all of these events was quite easy to get&nbsp;
it wrong. Although there were a few downsides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:08:01.650
web forms were generally successful to get site&nbsp;
up and running quickly. Couple of years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:11.130
in 2009, Microsoft released the first version&nbsp;
of ASP NET MVC, and they tried to overcome most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:19.470
of the problems with webforms. It had emphasis&nbsp;
on separation of concern. MVC code is typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:26.970
