00:49:58.060 --> 00:50:04.030
we have seen the overview of how Application&nbsp;
flow. Let's understand middlewares and pipeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:04.030 --> 00:50:11.710
in much more detail. Whenever an HTTP request&nbsp;
comes in, something must handle that request.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:11.710 --> 00:50:19.720
So it eventually results in an HTTP response.&nbsp;
Those pieces of code that handles the request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.720 --> 00:50:27.190
and results in a response make up the request&nbsp;
pipeline, what we can do is configure this request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:27.190 --> 00:50:34.870
pipeline by adding middlewares, which are software&nbsp;
components that are assembled into an application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:34.870 --> 00:50:43.240
pipeline to handle request and response. So&nbsp;
typically, a browser is going to send a request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:43.240 --> 00:50:50.380
to your server. And that request is going to&nbsp;
be interpreted by the server, and handled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:50.380 --> 00:50:58.810
some piece of software. Now first, that request is&nbsp;
attached to something called as the context object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:58.810 --> 00:51:07.600
as a part of software that manages this context.&nbsp;
In our case, it would be ASP. NET Core middleware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:14.530
you can essentially Think of it as a pipeline,&nbsp;
which is a series of pipes, that is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:14.530 --> 00:51:22.240
determine what is going to happen to the context.&nbsp;
So first, the request is passed along the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.240 --> 00:51:29.920
pipe, and the first pipe interprets the context&nbsp;
and checks out the request and determines if there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:37.240
is some type of response needed and attach that&nbsp;
to the context. If there is no immediate response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:37.240 --> 00:51:44.080
that should be handed back to the server, then the&nbsp;
context is simply passed along to the next pipe in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:44.080 --> 00:51:52.330
the pipeline. This continues, again to the next&nbsp;
piece of middleware, whatever it might be, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:52.330 --> 00:52:00.220
goes on till it reaches the very last pipe. Now,&nbsp;
it is also possible that at end of the pipeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:00.220 --> 00:52:07.480
no response has been found. And that will cause&nbsp;
a 404, not found and written back to whoever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:07.480 --> 00:52:14.560
originated the request. However, it is possible&nbsp;
that in any one or more of these middlewares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.560 --> 00:52:21.730
there may be a response that needs to be passed&nbsp;
back. And it could happen in any of the pipes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:21.730 --> 00:52:28.270
So sometimes it could happen that middleware&nbsp;
would not pass the context along the next piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.270 --> 00:52:34.660
but rather says, Okay, I have a response&nbsp;
that I need to send back. But typically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:34.660 --> 00:52:39.910
your context will go all the way through&nbsp;
the pipeline to the end, by the last piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:39.910 --> 00:52:46.540
of middleware sends a response, which gets back&nbsp;
through the pipeline to the server, and then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.540 --> 00:52:54.340
server sends back the response to the browser.&nbsp;
This is a very simplified version of how request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.340 --> 00:53:00.640
works. Let me just walk you through that, again,&nbsp;
in a brief overview so that this makes complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.640 --> 00:53:08.470
sense. When the request comes in to the server.&nbsp;
The server then accesses the dotnet core framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:08.470 --> 00:53:15.910
and puts the request into a context object, the&nbsp;
context gets passed along to the middlewares. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:15.910 --> 00:53:22.300
if a middleware has a response anywhere along&nbsp;
the way, then it will attach that response to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:22.300 --> 00:53:29.440
the context object, and then pass that context&nbsp;
object back through the pipeline to the server,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.440 --> 00:53:36.910
and then the server sends back the response to the&nbsp;
browser. Now keep in mind, the order of pipeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:36.910 --> 00:53:44.950
is important. It always gets past from the first&nbsp;
to the last. A good example will be authentication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:44.950 --> 00:53:51.820
middleware. If the middleware component finds the&nbsp;
request is an authorized, it will not pass it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:51.820 --> 00:53:58.660
the next piece of the middleware, but it will&nbsp;
immediately return an unauthorized response. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:58.660 --> 00:54:05.680
is hence important that authentication middleware&nbsp;
is added before other components that shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:05.680 --> 00:54:13.060
be triggered. In case of an unauthorized request.&nbsp;
We will be adding more middlewares in our project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

