00:55:36.940 --> 00:55:43.570
dependency injection software design pattern,&nbsp;
which is a technique for achieving inversion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:43.570 --> 00:55:51.610
control between classes and their dependencies.&nbsp;
Now, you might be wondering what is IOC or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:51.610 --> 00:55:59.290
inversion of control container. IOC container is&nbsp;
a framework for implementing automatic dependency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:59.290 --> 00:56:07.060
injection. It manages object creation and&nbsp;
its lifetime and also injects dependencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.060 --> 00:56:16.180
to their class. IOC container creates an object&nbsp;
of the specified class and also inject all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:16.180 --> 00:56:24.280
dependency objects through constructor property or&nbsp;
method at runtime, and disposes it at appropriate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:32.710
time. This is done so that we do not have to&nbsp;
create and manage the objects manually. Support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:32.710 --> 00:56:41.740
for dependency injection is built into ASP dotnet&nbsp;
core. In ASP dotnet core, both framework services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:41.740 --> 00:56:49.360
and application services can be injected into&nbsp;
your classes, rather than being tightly coupled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:49.360 --> 00:56:58.090
dependency injection is a design pattern in&nbsp;
which a class or object has its dependent classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.090 --> 00:57:06.160
injected, rather than creating them directly.&nbsp;
dependency injection can help developers decouple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:06.160 --> 00:57:13.660
the different pieces of their application. Let's&nbsp;
try to see this with an example. We have Bob here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:13.660 --> 00:57:20.230
who has been thinking about going on hiking. So&nbsp;
he makes a list of all the supplies like maps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:20.230 --> 00:57:28.510
flashlights, protein bars, etc, and puts them in&nbsp;
a backpack. Now next day, when he goes on hiking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.510 --> 00:57:36.700
he takes the backpack with him. This pack acts&nbsp;
as a container. So during the hike, whenever he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:36.700 --> 00:57:43.750
needs anything, he takes it out of the container&nbsp;
and uses them. This is the simple concept that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:43.750 --> 00:57:50.980
you put items you will need in a container. And&nbsp;
when you need them, it already exists inside a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:59.020
container. Let's understand this in a way that&nbsp;
is more related to coding. Let's imagine our&nbsp;&nbsp;

